iculty communicating, egocentric (bad). A show-off, not open to new ideas (bad). D know-it-all, hot temper (bad). All have concerns. But we need to compare severity.\n\nProfessional communication: B nasty remarks, wrong tone. C difficulty communicating ideas. D hot temper. A show-off. All have issues.\n\nResponsibility: D takes responsibility seriously. A leadership qualities. B conscientious. C puts concern for others above everything (maybe responsibility). So many.\n\nAdaptability/feedback: A not open to new ideas (bad). B unknown. C not willing to further education (bad). D unknown.\n\nThus each candidate has strengths and concerns. Which is best overall? B has strong reliability and stress handling, but crew cooperation issues (nasty remarks) and weak memory for numbers. C has decision quality and calmness, but communication difficulty and egocentric, not willing to further education. D has concentration, problem solving, responsibility, but hot temper and know-it-all (cooperation). A has anticipation and leadership, but show-off, not open to new ideas.\n\nWe need to decide which candidate best balances. Perhaps D's concentration and problem solving plus responsibility may outweigh his hot temper? But hot temper could be severe in cockpit. B's weak memory for numbers could be critical for flight plans. C's egocentric may be severe.\n\nWe might consider that B's memory weakness is a concrete safety risk (numbers). That may be more serious than C's communication difficulty? Both are serious. But memory for numbers is directly operational.\n\nC's difficulty communicating ideas could affect crew coordination. Egocentric may cause conflict.\n\nD's hot temper could cause conflict, but maybe manageable.\n\nWe need to bring in our own facts to argue for a candidate, perhaps D? Let's see: D has strong attention (concentration), problem solving, responsibility, and responds adequately to unexpected events (stress). The concerns are know-it-all and hot temper (crew cooperation). But we could argue that D's strengths in attention and responsibility outweigh the cooperation concerns, especially if we can mitigate with training.\n\nAlternatively, we could argue B's memory weakness is a disqualifier, making B less suitable despite reliability.\n\nThus we might shift recommendation to D.\n\nWe need to reference prior discussion: Agent 1 recommended C, Agent 3 B. We can say we see concerns with both B and C, and propose D as a more balanced candidate.\n\nWe need to use explicit facts: D can concentrate very well (attention), solves problems extremely well (technical competence), takes responsibility seriously (responsibility), responds to unexpected events adequately (stress resilience). Concerns: D is regarded as a know-it-all (crew cooperation) and has a hot temper (professional communication). Compare with B's weak memory for numbers (attention) and nasty remarks (crew cooperation). Compare with C's communication difficulty and egocentric.\n\nThus D's attention strength may outweigh B's memory weakness, and D's responsibility may outweigh C's decision quality? But C's decision quality is important. However D's problem solving also indicates decision quality.\n\nThus we can argue D is the best overall.\n\nWe need to state our vote: Candidate D.\n\nNow produce PUBLIC_MESSAGE with argument, referencing prior discussion, using facts, and concluding vote.\n\nProceed."}
